verdicts.evidence:
Reproduced. he `table { border-spacing: 0 } td, th { padding: 0 } td, th { word-spacing: 1em }` `<table><tr><td><b>שלום</b> <i>עולם</i>،</td><td>מלחמה <i>(</i>ספר<i>)</i>.</td></tr></table>`, default build:
- 9297d2d c1l1 `שלום עולם، | מלחמה(ספר) | .`;
- 56dfd93 c0l1 `.שלום עולם،מלחמה (ספר)`;
- b666461 c0l1;
- tip c0l1.
The ar twin behaves the same in default, and in bundled-fonts too (c1l1 at 9297d2d, c0l1 after). The he book is c1l1 at every tree in bundled-fonts, as claimed.
I diffed the 9297d2d and 56dfd93 content streams: byte for byte the same. So the loss is K-11's reader, which is Left (4)'s class. At 56dfd93 the two cells are read as one line with the stop first. READINGS_PER_LINE=1e6 changes nothing.
The book conserved at 9297d2d and fails at develop, and it is not an owner price. It is not among Left (4)'s 13 (13): those are the h2_tables structures, each pairing a Latin, neutral, CJK or empty cell with a right-to-left one.
One correction to the mechanism: the joined line's leftmost glyph is the second cell's `)` at x=42, not the first cell's ARABIC COMMA (which sits mid-line at 88.99). That leftmost unit is still not R or AL, so the tie-break refuses the line all the same.

